- Story & Context: Years after the Great Totality, Old Man Vesper stood on the jagged cliffs of the Eclipse Coast, cold sea-salt encrusting his eyelashes as he stared at a horizon that had permanently lost its color. Legend says the final, emerald optical flash of the sun’s reappearance was captured and swallowed by the guild's massive, vacuum-tube amplifiers, leaving the coastal sky forever gray. The locals still visit the rusted wreckage of the carnival wagons, claiming that if you press your ear to the iron casing of the dead sampler, you can hear a low-frequency hum vibrating with a brilliant green light. From the salt-flats below, the ghost-choir of the old guild seems to echo this myth, chanting their eternal boast: "We took the color and we locked it in the beat."
